Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||10:10 AM||||How old are you and how long have you been driving?<P> I just got my '85SE V6 running like its suppose to, and its pretty quick just the way it is.<P>  In fact I put the car into a spin just the other night, the road was a little wet from earlier rain, and I punched it making a left turn, started going around the corner sideways - totally unexpected.<P>  Before you get HP fever, get a GT V6, get it running well, and then decide if you want to 're-wire that sucker' Snort Snort Snort&lt;- Tim Allen impression&gt;||||reg||1
Z||000005||DavidHenshaw||05-24-2001||05:14 PM||||Gotta say I agree with Ken. I have an 86 GT fastback and the V6 has 214,000 miles on it but I'm still amazed at the accerlation. Yes another 40 hp would be nice but where would I use it. I was up to 110 mph in a heartbeat in my car once and the idea of 140 scares me.<P>BTW I was at bike week in Myrtle Beach S.C. last week and saw a cycle with a V8 rated at 607 hp. Thats on two wheels, give me a break!<P>Dave<P>86 GT<BR>Dave<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||05-24-2001||08:20 PM||||I guesss i am the weird on on the forum...<BR>suspension <BR>suspension<BR>suspension...<P>Going fast in a straight line is nice, but before I even thought of getting more horsepower, I wanted to be able to plant the power I already had as much as possible...<P>With a bigger motor you can go faster in the straights, with a better suspension you can go faster on the entire track (assuming the track has turns).<P>Yes a stock Fiero handles OK, but after tweaking the suspension, the Fiero is a different animal.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72 Race Director<BR>#47 SM<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">    <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
